{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Brinicle Descent: Microbial Rain from a Sinking Ice Finger",
    "date": "2026-10-06",
    "lesson": "When super-chilled, super-salty brine drains out of forming sea ice, it sinks through the warmer water below and freezes a hollow icicle around itself called a brinicle. As the brinicle descends through the McMurdo Sound water column, it scavenges and concentrates psychrophilic bacteria and ice algae from the brine channels overhead, flushing them downward in a plume of frigid, hyper-saline, nutrient-rich water. This scenario puts your founder at the base of a growing brinicle: the chemistry is brutal (sub-zero, hypersaline) but the food pulse is real, concentrated carbohydrate-rich algal exudate funneled straight down from the ice. The lesson is about extremophile physiology under combined cold and osmotic stress, and about a transient, structured food-delivery event rather than steady background grazing.",
    "citation": "Lewis, E.W. et al., brinicle formation observations, Antarctica (2011 BBC/Lake Vostok-adjacent studies); Cottier et al. on sea-ice brine drainage dynamics",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Brinicle ('ice finger of death') formation in McMurdo Sound/Antarctic fast ice, a real filmed phenomenon that flushes brine-channel microbes into the water column below"
  },
